from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(9,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("electric-charge-and-electric-current","Ravi rubbed a plastic comb on his hair and it picked up tiny paper bits. \"Electric charge!\" said his teacher.",
 ["Matter contains positive and negative charges. An atom that gains extra electrons becomes a negative ion. The charge of one electron, 1.6 x 10^-19 coulomb, is the fundamental unit of charge, and the total charge of a system is the algebraic sum of all its charges. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract, and this force is the electric force.",
  "Electric current is the rate at which charge flows past a point in a circuit. By convention it is taken as the flow of positive charge, called conventional current. A cell or battery supplies the electromotive force (emf), which is the work done in driving a unit charge around the complete circuit. Potential difference is V = W / q.",
  "Resistance is the opposition a component offers to the flow of current. One ohm is the resistance when a potential difference of one volt drives one ampere. Good conductors have low resistance and resistors are components made to provide resistance. Electric current has heating, magnetic and chemical effects, and electricity can be dangerous if it is handled carelessly."],
 ["electric charge","electric current","potential difference","resistance","ohm","effects of current"],
 ("A charge of 10 C flows through a lamp in 5 s. Find the current.",["Current = charge / time.","10 C divided by 5 s.","Current = 2 A."]),
 ("Current flows only in metals as electrons.",["Conventional current is taken as the flow of positive charge.","Charges can also move in liquids and gases."]),
 ["Charge of an electron is 1.6 x 10^-19 C.","Current = charge / time.","V = W / q.","Resistance is measured in ohms."],
 ["m|The charge of one electron is about|1.6 x 10^-19 C|1 C|9.8 C|It is the fundamental unit of charge.","t|Like charges repel each other.|T|Yes.","m|The unit of resistance is|ohm|ampere|volt|One ohm = one volt per ampere.","t|Current is the rate of flow of charge.|T|I = q / t.","m|Potential difference is given by|W / q|q / W|W x q|Work done per unit charge.","s|What is a combination of more than one cell called?|battery|A battery is a group of cells.","m|Conventional current is the flow of|positive charge|neutrons|light|It is taken as positive charge flow.","t|Good conductors have very high resistance.|F|They have low resistance."])
C("magnetism-and-electromagnetism","Asha moved a magnet near a coil connected to a small bulb and the bulb glowed for a moment. \"Magnetism made electricity!\" she cried.",
 ["The region around a magnet where its influence is felt is the magnetic field. A magnetic field line is a curve whose tangent at any point gives the direction of the field, and magnetic flux is the number of field lines passing through an area. A current-carrying wire also produces a magnetic field around it, which is the magnetic effect of current.",
  "A conductor carrying current in a magnetic field feels a force, and its direction is given by Fleming's left hand rule. This force is used in the electric motor, which changes electrical energy into mechanical energy.",
  "When the magnetic flux linked with a closed coil changes, an emf is induced. This is electromagnetic induction, and Lenz's law says the induced current flows so as to oppose the change that causes it. The electric generator uses it to change mechanical energy into electrical energy.",
  "A transformer changes alternating voltage. A step-up transformer changes a low voltage to a high voltage and a step-down transformer changes a high voltage to a low voltage. Transformers work only with alternating current."],
 ["magnetic field","magnetic flux","Fleming's left hand rule","electric motor","electromagnetic induction","transformer"],
 ("A transformer has 100 primary turns and 500 secondary turns. Is it step-up or step-down?",["Compare the turns of the two coils.","The secondary has more turns than the primary.","So it is a step-up transformer."]),
 ("A magnet and a coil must always be moving apart to give current.",["Any change in magnetic flux through the coil induces an emf.","Moving towards or away both work."]),
 ["Current makes a magnetic field.","A motor turns electrical energy into motion.","A generator works by induction.","Transformers step voltage up or down."],
 ["m|The rule for the force on a current-carrying conductor is|Fleming's left hand rule|Boyle's law|Ohm's law|It gives the direction of force.","t|An electric motor changes electrical energy into mechanical energy.|T|Yes.","m|Producing emf by changing magnetic flux is called|electromagnetic induction|conduction|reflection|Faraday's discovery.","t|A step-up transformer lowers the voltage.|F|It raises the voltage.","m|An electric generator changes|mechanical energy to electrical energy|heat to light|sound to heat|It uses induction.","s|Whose law says induced current opposes the change causing it?|Lenz;Lenz's law|Lenz's law gives the direction.","m|Transformers work with|alternating current|direct current only|water|They need changing flux.","t|A magnetic field line shows the direction of the field.|T|Yes."])
C("light","Meena saw her face upside-down in the back of a shiny spoon. \"Why is it inverted?\" she asked.",
 ["Optics is the branch of physics that deals with light. In reflection from a plane mirror the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the image is virtual, erect, of the same size and laterally inverted. Spherical mirrors are of two kinds: the concave mirror, which curves inwards, and the convex mirror, which curves outwards.",
  "A concave mirror can form real or virtual images depending on the position of the object, and it is used as a shaving mirror and in headlights. A convex mirror always gives a smaller, virtual, erect image with a wide field of view, which is why it is used as a rear-view mirror.",
  "The bending of light when it passes obliquely from one transparent medium to another is refraction. The ratio of the sine of the angle of incidence to the sine of the angle of refraction is the refractive index, and it has no unit. When light goes from a denser to a rarer medium at an angle beyond the critical angle, total internal reflection occurs, which makes diamonds sparkle and stars twinkle."],
 ["reflection","real and virtual image","concave mirror","convex mirror","refraction","total internal reflection"],
 ("A ray strikes a plane mirror at 30 degrees to the normal. What is the angle of reflection?",["The law of reflection says i = r.","i = 30 degrees.","So the angle of reflection is 30 degrees."]),
 ("A convex mirror is used as a shaving mirror.",["Shaving mirrors are concave because they magnify the face.","Convex mirrors give a wide, smaller view and are used for vehicle rear-view."]),
 ["Reflection: angle i = angle r.","Concave mirrors converge, convex diverge.","Refraction is bending of light.","Total internal reflection needs the critical angle."],
 ["m|In a plane mirror the image is|virtual and erect|real and inverted|very large|It cannot be caught on a screen.","t|The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.|T|That is the law of reflection.","m|A rear-view mirror is a|convex mirror|concave mirror|plane mirror|It gives a wide field of view.","t|The refractive index has a unit.|F|It is a ratio of similar quantities.","m|Bending of light between media is called|refraction|reflection|dispersion|It happens at the boundary.","s|Which effect makes diamonds sparkle? Two words.|total internal reflection|It traps light inside.","m|The angle of incidence for which refraction angle is 90 degrees is the|critical angle|normal|focal angle|Beyond it light is totally reflected.","t|Light travels faster than sound.|T|Yes."])
C("heat","Raju noticed that the metal spoon in hot tea became hot but the wooden handle of the ladle did not. \"Heat travels better in metal,\" he said.",
 ["The sum of the kinetic and potential energy of the molecules is the internal energy, and when it flows out it is called heat energy. Temperature is the degree of hotness or coolness of a body. The scales are Celsius, Fahrenheit and Kelvin, with F = (9/5)C + 32 and K = C + 273.15. Absolute zero is the temperature at which pressure and volume of a gas theoretically reach zero.",
  "Heat moves in three ways. In conduction it flows through solids from the hotter to the cooler part without the molecules moving. In convection it is carried by the movement of liquids and gases. In radiation it travels as electromagnetic waves and needs no medium, as the Sun's heat does.",
  "Heat capacity is the heat needed to raise the temperature of the whole body by 1 degree. Specific heat capacity is the heat needed to raise the temperature of unit mass by 1 degree, using Q = m c (change in T). When a substance changes state, such as ice melting, it takes in latent heat without a change in temperature."],
 ["internal energy","conduction","convection","radiation","specific heat capacity","latent heat"],
 ("Convert 100 degrees Celsius to kelvin.",["Use K = C + 273.15.","K = 100 + 273.15.","K = 373.15 K."]),
 ("Heat from the Sun reaches us by conduction.",["Between Sun and Earth there is almost no matter.","The heat reaches us by radiation."]),
 ["Heat flows from hot to cold.","Three modes: conduction, convection, radiation.","Q = m c (change in T).","Latent heat changes state, not temperature."],
 ["m|Heat transfer in solids without molecules moving is|conduction|convection|radiation|Energy passes from molecule to molecule.","t|Radiation needs a material medium.|F|It can cross empty space.","m|The Kelvin scale is also called the|absolute scale|Fahrenheit scale|metric scale|Zero kelvin is absolute zero.","t|Boiling point of water is 373.15 K.|T|100 + 273.15.","m|Specific heat capacity is heat needed to raise temperature of|unit mass by 1 degree|any body by 10 degrees|water only|That is the definition.","s|Heat absorbed during change of state with no temperature change is called|latent heat|Latent means hidden.","m|Hot air rising shows|convection|conduction|radiation|It moves with the fluid.","t|Internal energy is the sum of kinetic and potential energies of molecules.|T|Yes."])
